    title: 'Next Time: Chapter 27 — Flight from Thornfield',
    preview: 'In the depths of night, Jane makes her escape from Thornfield. She leaves while Rochester sleeps, taking almost nothing, knowing if she sees him again her resolve might break. She has no money, no plan, no destination—just the certainty that staying would destroy her. She travels as far as her small savings allow, then walks until she collapses, hungry and exhausted, on the moors. She\'s left everything—Rochester, home, security, even Adèle—to preserve her integrity. She faces potential death (exposure, starvation) rather than compromise her principles. Jane wanders the moors for days, begging for food, sleeping rough, becoming increasingly desperate. Just when she\'s about to die of exposure and starvation, she stumbles upon a house with lights in the windows: Moor House, where the Rivers family lives. She collapses on their doorstep. They take her in, nurse her back to health, and Jane begins the next phase of her journey: discovering her own family connections, finding purpose independent of Rochester, and becoming economically independent through surprising inheritance. But she never stops loving Rochester, never stops thinking of him, never stops wondering if leaving was right choice or terrible mistake.',
